1
00:00:02,116 --> 00:00:05,516
So that was the knapsack problem and our

2
00:00:05,869 --> 00:00:07,344
algorithm to solve it.

3
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:11,338
And that was hopefully also a step-by-step

4
00:00:11,899 --> 00:00:15,090
walkthrough that at least gets you started

5
00:00:15,187 --> 00:00:18,362
with finding your own solutions for problems

6
00:00:18,458 --> 00:00:20,880
you might be facing. Now, speaking of

7
00:00:21,056 --> 00:00:24,456
solutions, let's talk about greedy versus

8
00:00:24,568 --> 00:00:27,408
dynamic algorithms. And at least the term

9
00:00:27,601 --> 00:00:30,459
greedy is something you haven't heard thus

10
00:00:30,491 --> 00:00:31,278
far in this course.

11
00:00:32,273 --> 00:00:35,003
So what are greedy and what are dynamic

12
00:00:35,388 --> 00:00:37,459
algorithms or solutions?

13
00:00:38,679 --> 00:00:41,232
Well, let's start with greedy solutions,

14
00:00:41,329 --> 00:00:43,962
greedy approaches to solving a problem.

15
00:00:44,861 --> 00:00:47,574
A greedy approach is a solution, an

16
00:00:47,671 --> 00:00:51,010
algorithm you write where you basically try

17
00:00:51,058 --> 00:00:53,064
to make the best possible possible decision

18
00:00:53,289 --> 00:00:55,999
in every step. So, for example, in every

19
00:00:56,192 --> 00:00:57,122
loop iteration,

20
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:00,731
and you hope that that altogether leads to

21
00:01:01,068 --> 00:01:03,923
an overall best solution. And I'll show you

22
00:01:04,292 --> 00:01:06,858
a possible greedy approach to solve the

23
00:01:06,858 --> 00:01:09,617
knapsack problem so that you get an idea for

24
00:01:09,681 --> 00:01:12,232
such a greedy algorithm in just a second.

25
00:01:13,258 --> 00:01:15,279
Now, the good thing about such greedy

26
00:01:15,424 --> 00:01:18,728
algorithms is that they're often faster to

27
00:01:18,728 --> 00:01:20,139
set up and to come up with than

28
00:01:20,732 --> 00:01:23,201
But they also don't necessarily provide the

29
00:01:23,217 --> 00:01:26,760
best runtime and/or result. You might also

30
00:01:26,824 --> 00:01:29,870
come up with a greedy algorithm that looks

31
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:31,938
like it does the right thing, but that

32
00:01:32,018 --> 00:01:34,583
actually doesn't find the best solution.

33
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:37,500
And that's the advantage of dynamic

34
00:01:37,628 --> 00:01:40,818
algorithms. Now dynamic is a term we already

35
00:01:40,834 --> 00:01:43,415
heard in this course when I talked about

36
00:01:43,415 --> 00:01:45,820
dynamic programming, which in the end meant

37
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:47,968
that we had some memoization going on. And

38
00:01:48,048 --> 00:01:50,100
that's exactly what dynamic means here.

39
00:01:50,693 --> 00:01:54,173
A dynamic algorithm is an algorithm where

40
00:01:54,382 --> 00:01:56,772
you most likely are using memoization

41
00:01:57,125 --> 00:01:59,018
because you're doing one very important

42
00:01:59,034 --> 00:01:59,098
thing.

43
00:01:59,916 --> 00:02:03,783
You evaluate all possible solutions to find

44
00:02:03,783 --> 00:02:06,847
the overall best solution by then comparing

45
00:02:07,055 --> 00:02:09,799
all those individual solutions. And that's

46
00:02:09,799 --> 00:02:12,173
why you typically also want memoization

47
00:02:12,205 --> 00:02:15,141
because if you evaluate all possible

48
00:02:15,173 --> 00:02:18,301
solutions, you wanna avoid duplicate work.

49
00:02:18,783 --> 00:02:21,400
Now, in dynamic algorithms, or when using

50
00:02:21,512 --> 00:02:24,129
dynamic programming, you therefore typically

51
00:02:24,242 --> 00:02:27,116
do something which is also called divide and

52
00:02:27,212 --> 00:02:29,508
conquer. You're following a divide and

53
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:30,471
conquer approach.

54
00:02:31,065 --> 00:02:33,955
And this is also a new term, in the end just

55
00:02:33,955 --> 00:02:36,572
a term for describing dynamic algorithms,

56
00:02:36,765 --> 00:02:39,976
dynamic programming. The idea behind divide

57
00:02:39,976 --> 00:02:42,850
and conquer approaches is that you divide a

58
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:45,482
problem into smaller, easy-to-solve

59
00:02:45,578 --> 00:02:46,364
subproblems.

60
00:02:46,957 --> 00:02:48,866
And that's what we did with the knapsack

61
00:02:48,866 --> 00:02:51,913
problem, for example. We in the end used a

62
00:02:51,913 --> 00:02:54,976
divide-and-conquer recursive approach to

63
00:02:54,976 --> 00:02:58,071
split our problem into subproblems, and then

64
00:02:58,103 --> 00:03:00,958
we evaluated all possible combinations to

65
00:03:00,974 --> 00:03:02,593
find the best combination.

66
00:03:03,556 --> 00:03:06,041
That of course means that writing such a

67
00:03:06,041 --> 00:03:09,297
dynamic algorithm is a bit more complex. You

68
00:03:09,313 --> 00:03:12,137
have to come up with how to best split the

69
00:03:12,137 --> 00:03:14,721
problem and how to best then compare the

70
00:03:14,769 --> 00:03:17,674
different solutions. But ultimately, you are

71
00:03:17,674 --> 00:03:20,209
guaranteed to find the best possible

72
00:03:20,242 --> 00:03:22,553
solution, of course, because you're having a

73
00:03:22,553 --> 00:03:24,880
look at all solutions. So of course you can

74
00:03:24,976 --> 00:03:25,843
find the best one.

75
00:03:26,404 --> 00:03:29,357
And often, despite looking at all solutions,

76
00:03:29,775 --> 00:03:33,129
because you're using memoization, you might

77
00:03:33,305 --> 00:03:35,649
also have a better runtime than with a

78
00:03:35,649 --> 00:03:36,499
greedy algorithm.

79
00:03:37,498 --> 00:03:39,527
So now let's have a look at a greedy

80
00:03:39,575 --> 00:03:41,798
algorithm though. Let's see how we could try

81
00:03:41,927 --> 00:03:44,939
to solve the knapsack problem we solved with

82
